1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:13,731
The tender offer went out on the fourth of January,
at four dollars and ten cents a share

2
00:00:13,751 --> 00:00:19,616
— thirty-four percent above the price Verdant Axis
had closed at on the last trading day

3
00:00:19,636 --> 00:00:25,167
of the year, and fifty percent below where it had
been before Hannah Price's story.

4
00:00:25,736 --> 00:00:32,195
It was, the financial press agreed, an opportunistic
offer. It was also, the financial

5
00:00:32,215 --> 00:00:35,359
press agreed, very probably going to work.

6
00:00:35,929 --> 00:00:41,910
On the ninth of January, Curtis Vane was indicted in
federal court in Baltimore on eleven

7
00:00:41,930 --> 00:00:48,114
counts of healthcare fraud. On the tenth, three of
Verdant's nine directors received letters

8
00:00:48,134 --> 00:00:54,183
from shareholder lawyers. On the twelfth, the
company's auditors announced that they would

9
00:00:54,203 --> 00:00:58,296
be, quote, unable to sign the annual
accounts pending review.

10
00:00:58,866 --> 00:01:04,564
By the fifteenth, holders of thirty-eight percent of
the company had tendered their shares

11
00:01:04,584 --> 00:01:09,964
to Umbrella. The board called a special meeting for
the nineteenth, to decide whether

12
00:01:09,984 --> 00:01:12,506
to recommend the offer to everyone else.

13
00:01:13,075 --> 00:01:18,429
Catherine Adeyemi spent the week before the meeting
on the phone. She spoke to all nine

14
00:01:18,449 --> 00:01:23,740
directors, separately. She did not threaten any of
them. She simply explained, to each

15
00:01:23,760 --> 00:01:29,052
one, in the calm voice of a woman who had already
done the maths on their behalf, what

16
00:01:29,072 --> 00:01:34,487
the next two years of their lives would look like if
the offer failed — the depositions,

17
00:01:34,507 --> 00:01:40,045
the insurance claims, the headlines — and what they
would look like if it succeeded, which

18
00:01:40,065 --> 00:01:40,724
was: quiet.

19
00:01:41,295 --> 00:01:46,353
By the eighteenth, eight of the nine had told her,
off the record, which way they would

20
00:01:46,373 --> 00:01:46,644
vote.

21
00:01:47,215 --> 00:01:49,159
The ninth was Graham Tolliver.

22
00:01:51,228 --> 00:01:57,006
The Verdant Axis boardroom was on the twelfth
floor, behind glass, with a view of the

23
00:01:57,026 --> 00:02:03,145
river and, on the far bank, if you knew where to look,
a small brick building with a faded

24
00:02:03,165 --> 00:02:03,486
sign.

25
00:02:04,055 --> 00:02:09,959
The meeting was called for ten. At ten, eight
directors, two lawyers, Catherine Adeyemi,

26
00:02:09,979 --> 00:02:15,949
and Mister Pell were seated at the long table. Graham
Tolliver was standing at the window

27
00:02:15,969 --> 00:02:17,699
with his back to the room.

28
00:02:18,270 --> 00:02:20,205
Albert Wesker was not there.

29
00:02:20,776 --> 00:02:24,992
At ten past ten, Tolliver turned
around and began to speak.

30
00:02:26,182 --> 00:02:29,811
I'll say what everyone's thinking. He's late. The man
who wants to own this company can't

31
00:02:29,831 --> 00:02:33,337
be bothered to show up for the meeting where we
decide whether to sell it to him. That

32
00:02:33,357 --> 00:02:36,822
tells you everything. He's a hustler. He came out
of nowhere, he fed a hit piece to a

33
00:02:36,842 --> 00:02:40,348
local rag, he's got a licence with a company in
Shenzhen, and he's got a basement full

34
00:02:40,368 --> 00:02:44,161
of — nobody even knows what. And you want to hand him a
hundred and forty jobs and a pipeline

35
00:02:44,181 --> 00:02:45,554
that took me eight years to build?

36
00:02:46,474 --> 00:02:49,166
Nobody answered him. So he kept talking.

37
00:02:49,736 --> 00:02:55,672
This is the great secret of the powerful, and it is
not complicated. When a room is silent,

38
00:02:55,692 --> 00:03:01,366
the frightened man fills it. He fills it with
arguments, and then with accusations, and

39
00:03:01,386 --> 00:03:06,928
then — because the silence is still there, and he
cannot bear it — with things he did

40
00:03:06,948 --> 00:03:07,975
not mean to say.

41
00:03:08,665 --> 00:03:13,144
That data was clean. The trial was clean. Curtis
made some mistakes with the paperwork,

42
00:03:13,164 --> 00:03:17,746
fine, but the drug works. It works. And if anyone at
this table had had the guts to stand

43
00:03:17,766 --> 00:03:20,848
behind it instead of reading the
Wilmington goddamn Ledger —

44
00:03:21,549 --> 00:03:25,411
— and I'll tell you something else. I knew about the
sites. Okay? I knew the numbers looked

45
00:03:25,431 --> 00:03:29,038
good. Everyone knew. That's what you do. That's the
business. Everybody at this table

46
00:03:29,058 --> 00:03:29,293
knew —

47
00:03:31,014 --> 00:03:32,051
He stopped.

48
00:03:32,622 --> 00:03:38,061
One of the lawyers had very slowly put down his pen.
Two of the directors were looking

49
00:03:38,081 --> 00:03:43,521
at each other. Catherine Adeyemi, at the far end of
the table, had not moved a muscle,

50
00:03:43,541 --> 00:03:48,917
but had — the lawyer next to her would say later —
the expression of a woman watching

51
00:03:48,937 --> 00:03:52,852
someone walk into a glass door she had
spent a week polishing.

52
00:03:54,423 --> 00:03:55,629
The door opened.

53
00:03:56,199 --> 00:04:02,009
Albert Wesker walked in. He wore a black coat, and
black gloves, and his sunglasses, and

54
00:04:02,029 --> 00:04:07,640
he did not apologise for being late, and he did not
take a seat. He walked the length

55
00:04:07,660 --> 00:04:13,404
of the room, without hurrying, while nine people
watched him, and stopped at the end of

56
00:04:13,424 --> 00:04:16,318
the table directly opposite
Graham Tolliver.

57
00:04:16,889 --> 00:04:21,991
He had been standing outside the glass door for
eleven minutes. He had heard everything.

58
00:04:24,060 --> 00:04:30,141
He did not speak for a while. The silence came back,
heavier than before. Tolliver opened

59
00:04:30,161 --> 00:04:32,266
his mouth, and closed it again.

60
00:04:32,836 --> 00:04:34,797
Then Wesker said eleven words.

61
00:04:36,286 --> 00:04:40,566
You'll resign today, Graham. The only
question is how you're remembered.

62
00:04:42,286 --> 00:04:44,312
And then he took off his glasses.

63
00:04:44,882 --> 00:04:50,521
What Graham Tolliver saw in the Director's eyes in
that moment, he never described to

64
00:04:50,541 --> 00:04:56,381
anyone. The other people at the table saw nothing
unusual — a pair of pale, cold, rather

65
00:04:56,401 --> 00:05:02,041
beautiful eyes, and a steady gaze. But Tolliver was
standing directly opposite, eight

66
00:05:02,061 --> 00:05:07,767
feet away, at the angle of the winter light off the
river, and he saw something in the

67
00:05:07,787 --> 00:05:13,161
pupils that his brain refused to finish
processing, and his hands began to shake.

68
00:05:13,732 --> 00:05:19,682
It was not a threat. There had been no threat. It
was worse. It was the absolute, total

69
00:05:19,702 --> 00:05:25,172
calm of a man who has already won, looking at a
man who has only just found out.

70
00:05:27,362 --> 00:05:28,445
I resign.

71
00:05:29,963 --> 00:05:36,033
Nobody spoke. One of the directors began, very
quietly, to cry. The lawyer picked his

72
00:05:36,053 --> 00:05:38,611
pen back up and wrote down the time.

73
00:05:39,182 --> 00:05:44,904
The board of Verdant Axis Therapeutics voted eight
to nothing to recommend the Umbrella

74
00:05:44,924 --> 00:05:48,072
offer to its shareholders. It
took four minutes.

75
00:05:49,641 --> 00:05:54,912
Graham Tolliver walked out of the building he had
built with nothing in his hands. He

76
00:05:54,932 --> 00:06:00,264
was never charged with anything. He did not need to
be. He became, for the rest of his

77
00:06:00,284 --> 00:06:05,679
career, a sentence in other people's stories — the
man who admitted it in the boardroom

78
00:06:05,699 --> 00:06:10,970
— and he would live another thirty-one years, in a
comfortable house, with a drinking

79
00:06:10,990 --> 00:06:16,260
problem, telling anyone who would listen that the
man with the glasses had not been a

80
00:06:16,280 --> 00:06:16,944
man at all.

81
00:06:17,516 --> 00:06:20,334
Nobody listened. Not for a long time.

82
00:06:22,903 --> 00:06:28,755
When the room had emptied — when even Catherine had
gone to call her partners, and Pell

83
00:06:28,775 --> 00:06:34,964
had gone to call the bank — the air in the boardroom of
Verdant Axis lit up like a cathedral

84
00:06:34,984 --> 00:06:35,437
window.

85
00:06:36,757 --> 00:06:40,980
Corporate conquest. Verdant Axis
Therapeutics.

86
00:06:45,831 --> 00:06:47,083
Level six.

87
00:06:49,303 --> 00:06:50,286
Seven.

88
00:06:52,574 --> 00:06:53,386
Eight.

89
00:06:55,843 --> 00:06:56,797
Nine.

90
00:06:59,113 --> 00:07:00,331
Level ten.

91
00:07:03,553 --> 00:07:09,707
He felt each one. They went through him like a series
of doors opening in a long corridor

92
00:07:09,727 --> 00:07:15,602
— each one letting in more air, more light, more
room. His body was already more than

93
00:07:15,622 --> 00:07:21,429
human. Now, level by level, it remembered how much
more it had been built to become.

94
00:07:21,999 --> 00:07:27,702
He placed the points standing up, in the empty
boardroom, with the river going grey behind

95
00:07:27,722 --> 00:07:27,957
him.

96
00:07:29,276 --> 00:07:30,348
Status.

97
00:07:35,516 --> 00:07:41,823
Charisma thirty-four. Ten points beyond the very edge
of what a human voice had ever done

98
00:07:41,843 --> 00:07:48,008
to a human crowd. The Adolf half of the mind looked
at that number for a long time, the

99
00:07:48,028 --> 00:07:50,567
way a starving man looks at a table.

100
00:07:52,637 --> 00:07:55,004
The first thing he bought was his freedom.

101
00:07:56,324 --> 00:08:03,211
Purchase confirmed. Viral stability, one
hundred percent. Permanent.

102
00:08:04,800 --> 00:08:09,588
The last of the tightness under his sternum
went away, and did not come back.

103
00:08:10,158 --> 00:08:16,241
In his old life he had needed a serum, measured to
the milligram, injected on a schedule,

104
00:08:16,261 --> 00:08:22,069
to keep the virus from eating him alive. Here and
now, standing in the boardroom of a

105
00:08:22,089 --> 00:08:28,104
company he had just taken, he felt the Prototype
settle into him like a key into a lock.

106
00:08:28,124 --> 00:08:32,629
It was not a leash anymore. It was not a
clock. It was simply him.

107
00:08:33,320 --> 00:08:33,917
There.

108
00:08:34,437 --> 00:08:37,059
You are finished being careful, then.

109
00:08:37,578 --> 00:08:40,908
I'm finished being mortal. Careful is
a different subject.

110
00:08:42,927 --> 00:08:44,965
He did the ten-pull in the basement.

111
00:08:45,534 --> 00:08:51,474
Nobody told him to. It was not rational. But it
was, the Adolf half insisted, correct

112
00:08:51,494 --> 00:08:57,644
— to stand before the sleeping thing that fate had
already given them, and ask fate what

113
00:08:57,664 --> 00:08:58,485
else it had.

114
00:08:59,806 --> 00:09:00,860
Ten draws.

115
00:09:07,671 --> 00:09:08,768
Results.

116
00:09:21,380 --> 00:09:25,428
Office supplies. A car. A
receptionist. A coffee machine.

117
00:09:26,119 --> 00:09:28,737
Fate has a sense of humour.

118
00:09:29,256 --> 00:09:31,899
Variance has a sense of humour.
Keep reading.

119
00:09:32,419 --> 00:09:38,831
The deep identity would matter enormously. It
thickened his invented past — school records

120
00:09:38,851 --> 00:09:45,120
in Switzerland, tax returns, a mother's grave in a
cemetery outside Basel. It would make

121
00:09:45,140 --> 00:09:51,623
his history perfect. It was, in fact, the first
mistake the System ever made on his behalf,

122
00:09:51,643 --> 00:09:57,984
because three years later an FBI agent would find
that history, and notice precisely that

123
00:09:58,004 --> 00:09:59,056
it was perfect.

124
00:10:00,875 --> 00:10:09,698
Epic. Silent security squad. Epic. Concealed
sublevel. Designation, Arklay.

125
00:10:11,288 --> 00:10:12,350
Arklay.

126
00:10:12,919 --> 00:10:17,180
The System had not asked him what to call
it. It had simply known.

127
00:10:17,751 --> 00:10:23,117
A containment laboratory, grade four — the highest
level of biosafety on Earth — built

128
00:10:23,137 --> 00:10:28,942
beneath the building, beneath the basement, beneath the
Tyrant. It would be there by morning,

129
00:10:28,962 --> 00:10:34,390
the panel said. It would be reached by a freight
elevator that officially went nowhere,

130
00:10:34,410 --> 00:10:39,776
and it would appear in no drawing, no permit, and no
satellite image. And its name was

131
00:10:39,796 --> 00:10:44,975
the name of the mountains where, in another world,
he had first become what he was.

132
00:10:45,663 --> 00:10:48,350
You are sentimental after all.

133
00:10:48,870 --> 00:10:51,174
It isn't sentiment. It's continuity.

134
00:10:54,693 --> 00:10:56,302
The tenth card was black.

135
00:10:56,873 --> 00:11:03,165
Not gold. Not violet. Black, with an edge of crimson
that seemed to burn without giving

136
00:11:03,185 --> 00:11:03,891
any light.

137
00:11:05,211 --> 00:11:10,204
Classification withheld.
Progenitor seed. Not yet.

138
00:11:13,293 --> 00:11:14,957
He read it three times.

139
00:11:15,528 --> 00:11:22,005
He knew that word. In his old world, the Progenitor was
the root of everything — the ancient

140
00:11:22,025 --> 00:11:28,432
flower in the African dark from which every virus
Umbrella ever made had grown. The source.

141
00:11:28,452 --> 00:11:29,916
The seed of the seed.

142
00:11:30,486 --> 00:11:36,148
And the System, which had never once refused him
anything, had just handed it to him,

143
00:11:36,168 --> 00:11:37,217
and told him no.

144
00:11:37,908 --> 00:11:38,740
Not yet.

145
00:11:39,260 --> 00:11:45,161
It was his own phrase. The one he had said to Evelyn
at the piano, and to the eye in the

146
00:11:45,181 --> 00:11:47,986
frost. The System had said it back to him.

147
00:11:49,557 --> 00:11:55,586
Behind him, in the grey machine, the compressor
cycled. A long, slow breath. A pause.

148
00:11:56,157 --> 00:12:01,898
For the second time in his new life, for half a
second, Albert Wesker was afraid. And

149
00:12:01,918 --> 00:12:03,932
then — as before — he was not.

150
00:12:06,501 --> 00:12:08,557
The new sign went up in February.

151
00:12:09,128 --> 00:12:14,859
Two of them, in fact. The first on the glass tower
on the north bank, where for eight

152
00:12:14,879 --> 00:12:20,611
years the green letters of Verdant Axis had been
lit every night, and where a crew in

153
00:12:20,631 --> 00:12:26,566
a cherry-picker now bolted a red-and-white emblem,
eight segments, like an umbrella seen

154
00:12:26,586 --> 00:12:28,663
from above, four metres across.

155
00:12:29,234 --> 00:12:34,776
The second over the glass doors of the small brick
building on the south bank, where the

156
00:12:34,796 --> 00:12:40,529
faded blue letters of Division Seven had been taken
down and replaced with the same emblem,

157
00:12:40,549 --> 00:12:43,058
smaller, and the single word beneath it.

158
00:12:43,628 --> 00:12:49,280
Walter Pruitt stood in the parking lot and watched
them put it up. When it was done, he

159
00:12:49,300 --> 00:12:55,018
laughed out loud — the first real laugh anyone at
Umbrella had heard from him — and went

160
00:12:55,038 --> 00:12:56,323
inside to tell Dana.

161
00:12:57,893 --> 00:13:03,064
Hannah Price photographed both signs for the Ledger.
She stood on the riverbank in the

162
00:13:03,084 --> 00:13:08,196
cold, and took the picture of the tower, and looked
at it on the little screen of her

163
00:13:08,216 --> 00:13:13,508
camera, and felt — she wrote in her notebook that
night — cold in a way that had nothing

164
00:13:13,528 --> 00:13:14,897
to do with the weather.

165
00:13:15,466 --> 00:13:21,069
She had written the story that made this possible. She
had checked every line of it. Every

166
00:13:21,089 --> 00:13:22,257
word had been true.

167
00:13:22,826 --> 00:13:27,737
It had not occurred to her, until she saw the
emblem go up, that the truth could be a

168
00:13:27,757 --> 00:13:30,638
weapon, and that someone else
could be holding it.

169
00:13:32,707 --> 00:13:38,394
That night, he drove home across the river, in a
black sedan that had been a Common roll,

170
00:13:38,414 --> 00:13:40,639
past a tower with his emblem on it.

171
00:13:41,208 --> 00:13:46,852
It was the whole argument. The one they had been
having for five months, in the car, in

172
00:13:46,872 --> 00:13:52,580
the dark, in the quiet of one skull. They had it one
more time, all the way through, and

173
00:13:52,600 --> 00:13:53,881
neither of them won.

174
00:13:54,572 --> 00:14:00,618
A people that believes. That is what you need. Not a
company. Not shareholders. Believers.

175
00:14:00,638 --> 00:14:06,549
Men and women who would die for the emblem on that
tower. You felt it in the break room.

176
00:14:06,569 --> 00:14:09,784
The System paid you for it. You
know I am right.

177
00:14:10,304 --> 00:14:15,862
A world that signs. That's what I need. Not
believers. Believers are loud. Believers get

178
00:14:15,882 --> 00:14:21,376
photographed. Believers write manifestos and make
the evening news. I want a world that

179
00:14:21,396 --> 00:14:26,890
signs every contract I put in front of it, and
doesn't understand the clause until it's

180
00:14:26,910 --> 00:14:27,713
already mine.

181
00:14:28,234 --> 00:14:30,283
Contracts can be broken.

182
00:14:30,802 --> 00:14:33,002
So can people. Contracts are cheaper.

183
00:14:34,522 --> 00:14:36,610
Then we disagree.

184
00:14:37,129 --> 00:14:40,605
We've disagreed since the bunker. It
hasn't stopped us yet.

185
00:14:42,625 --> 00:14:48,431
They agreed on only one thing, that night, crossing
the river. They did not need to say

186
00:14:48,451 --> 00:14:54,592
it aloud. It was simply there, underneath both of them,
the way the Prototype was underneath

187
00:14:54,612 --> 00:14:55,194
his skin.

188
00:14:55,764 --> 00:14:56,505
More.

189
00:14:59,076 --> 00:15:04,299
In five months, a man who had woken up with no
money, no name, and a virus eating him

190
00:15:04,319 --> 00:15:09,543
alive, had taken a dead laboratory, a building, a
rival, a trademark, and a tower. He

191
00:15:09,563 --> 00:15:14,972
had summoned an accountant, a guard, a janitor, and
six men who did not show up properly

192
00:15:14,992 --> 00:15:20,215
on camera. He had a monster in the basement and a
laboratory beneath the monster. And

193
00:15:20,235 --> 00:15:25,459
in a sealed box in the air that only he could see,
he had the seed of the seed, and a

194
00:15:25,479 --> 00:15:27,557
machine that had told him to wait.

195
00:15:28,126 --> 00:15:31,280
He was, by any measure, a
remarkable success.

196
00:15:31,850 --> 00:15:33,444
He had not yet begun.
